course: dictionary
lesson: 3430
title: Extreme: very great, or the furthest point
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Extreme» means very great, very strong, or much more than usual. «There was extreme heat in the desert at midday.» Test it: swap in «very hot» and the sentence still makes sense, so the meaning is right.
explain: Extreme also means far from the middle or from the usual. «Some microbes live in extreme places, such as boiling hot springs.» Those places are far from where most living things can survive, so the context tells you the meaning.
explain: As a noun, an extreme is the furthest point of something. «From one extreme to the other» means from one far end to the other far end. In «extreme sports», extreme means dangerous and exciting, so read the words around it to choose the right meaning.
fact: Extreme means very great, very strong, or much more than usual.
fact: «Extreme weather» includes droughts, floods and heatwaves.
fact: «Extreme sports» are dangerous, exciting sports.
fact: An extreme is the furthest point of something, as in «from one extreme to the other».
fact: On fire danger signs, «extreme» means the danger is at its highest, and no fires are allowed.
exercise: «The children had to stay indoors because of the extreme heat.» What does extreme heat mean? | Heat from a warm cup of tea | *Very great heat, much more than usual | A light, gentle breeze
exercise: Lia says extreme sports are slow and calm sports. Is she right? | *No, extreme sports are dangerous, exciting sports | Yes, because they are played indoors | Yes, they are slow and calm
exercise: What is an extreme? | The middle of something | *The furthest point of something | A small pet
test: «Some microbes live in extreme places, such as boiling hot springs.» What does extreme suggest about these places? | Cool, quiet gardens | *Far from the usual, very harsh places | Places with soft grass and shade
test: «Extreme weather is coming, with floods and heatwaves.» Which of these could count as extreme weather? | A gentle drizzle on a calm day | A soft breeze in spring | *A heatwave that lasts for weeks
exam: Jayden says extreme only means very far away. Is he right? | Yes, extreme always means very far away | No, it means very small and quiet | *No, it can also mean very great, very strong, or the furthest point
